@endnode

@node PROTOCOL "How a plugin works"
@{b}How a plugin works@{ub}

A plugin is an ordinary AmigaOS program.  When the user types its trigger
or prefix and presses Enter, Beam68 runs:

  "<plugin drawer>/MyPlugin" <INPUT-AS-HEX> "T:Beam68-<id>" BEAM68_REPLY <serial>

argv[1] is the text after the trigger, hex-encoded so it can never inject
Shell syntax ("-" when empty, at most 120 bytes). argv[2] is reserved for
ARexx text replies. argv[3] is BEAM68_REPLY; argv[4] is a decimal request
serial. Native plugins send up to eight selectable rows in one Exec message.
The manifest and message version are both 1, the first public protocol.

@endnode

@node LIBRARY "The SDK library"
@{b}beam68plugin.h@{ub}

  beam68_plugin_input(argc, argv, input, size)
      decodes argv[1]; returns 0 for a malformed call
  beam68_plugin_text(argc,argv, text)
  beam68_plugin_error(argc,argv, text)
      send one text row and return the exit code for main()
  beam68_trim, beam68_equal, beam68_prefix, beam68_contains, beam68_copy
      small case-insensitive ASCII helpers
  beam68_catalog_open("MyPlugin.catalog"), beam68_str(id, "English"),
  beam68_catalog_close()
      optional translations via locale.library; put catalogs in your
      drawer as Catalogs/<language>/MyPlugin.catalog

Example:

  static char input[BEAM68_PLUGIN_MAX_INPUT+1];
  if (!beam68_plugin_input(argc,argv,input,sizeof(input))) return 20;
  return beam68_plugin_text(argc,argv,"Hello!");

@endnode

@node ROWS "Selectable rows"
@{b}Protocol 1: selectable rows@{ub}

All native plugins declare VERSION=1 and receive:

  <plugin> <HEX> <reply-file> BEAM68_REPLY <serial>

Answer with up to 8 rows:

  beam68_rows_begin();
  beam68_row(BEAM68_ROW_OPEN, "Work:ReadMe", "Work:ReadMe");
  beam68_row(BEAM68_ROW_QUERY, "Search again", "find:readme");
  beam68_row(BEAM68_ROW_TEXT, "42 matches", NULL);
  return beam68_rows_send(argc, argv, 0);

OPEN opens the value via Workbench, QUERY puts the value in the search
field, TEXT copies the text to the clipboard.  The rows arrive as one
message on the public port BEAM68_REPLY. The launcher owns and frees the
public allocation. Delivery fails with exit code 20 if the port or memory is
unavailable; there is no file fallback for native replies. Rebuild pre-release
plugins with this SDK; experimental invocation formats are rejected.

@endnode

@node REXX "ARexx plugins"
@{b}ARexx plugins and live previews@{ub}

COMMAND=PLUGINDIR:MyPlugin.rexx makes the launcher run

  RX MyPlugin.rexx <HEX> <reply-file>

Decode with X2C(hex) ("-" means empty), write one TEXT= or ERROR= line to
<reply-file>.part and rename it.  See Example/HelloRexx.  ARexx plugins
use the Protocol 1 text transport and need RexxMast. Native plugins that
control ARexx hosts use row messages.

PREVIEW=YES runs the plugin ~400 ms after typing stops and shows the
answer under the query.  Keep previews fast and side-effect free.

@endnode

@node LAYOUT "Package layout"
@{b}Package layout@{ub}

A package is one drawer:

  HelloWorld/
    HelloWorld.plugin    the manifest (exactly one)
    HelloWorld           the program
    HelloWorld.info      optional icon
    HelloWorld.guide     optional help

It is installed as Beam68/Plugins/HelloWorld/.  In the manifest,
@{b}PLUGINDIR:@{ub} means this drawer.  When your program runs, its
PROGDIR: is this drawer too.

@endnode

@node MANIFEST "The manifest"
@{b}The manifest@{ub}

  VERSION=1                     first public protocol
  ID=org.example.helloworld     unique and permanent
  PLUGIN_VERSION=1.0            your version
  MIN_BEAM68=0.1                lowest Beam68 you support
  NAME=Hello World
  AUTHOR=Your Name
  LICENSE=MIT
  URL=https://example.org/
  TRIGGER=+                     one printable character
  PREFIX=HELLO:                 optional, one word
  COMMAND=PLUGINDIR:HelloWorld  required form for packages
  DESCRIPTION=Greets whoever you type.
  CATEGORY=Examples
  CAPABILITIES=READONLY
  EXAMPLE=hello:Amiga
  HELP=Type hello: and a name.
  ICON=PLUGINDIR:HelloWorld     optional (or PROGDIR:Beam68)
  GUIDE=PLUGINDIR:HelloWorld.guide  optional help, opened by the Help key
  SETTINGS=ENVARC:Beam68/Plugins/HelloWorld.cfg   optional
  SETTING=KEY|Label|TEXT|Default                  optional, max 6

COMMAND and ICON must stay inside the drawer: no ":", no leading "/", no
"//".  SETTINGS must be a flat file in ENVARC:Beam68/Plugins/ so it
survives updates.  SETTING types: TEXT, NUMBER, BOOL, CHOICE (choices
comma-separated after the default).

@endnode

@node CAPABILITIES "Capabilities"
@{b}Capabilities@{ub}

Declare what the plugin does; users see it before installing.

  READONLY    only reads information
  FILES       reads or writes files
  SHELL       runs AmigaDOS commands
  AREXX       sends ARexx messages
  CLIPBOARD   reads or changes the clipboard
  SYSTEM      may reboot, signal tasks or change the system;
              Beam68 asks for a second Enter before each run

@endnode

@node BUILD "Build and package"
@{b}Build and package@{ub}

  cd Example
  make vbcc          (or: make gcc)
  make package

package-plugin.py checks the manifest and that the program is an AmigaOS
executable, then writes Beam68-Plugin-<Name>-<version>.lha (or .zip if no
lha tool is installed) with exactly one top-level drawer.  Use --check to
validate only.

@endnode

@node INSTALL "How users install"
@{b}How users install@{ub}

In Beam68Prefs, Plugins page: drop the archive or drawer on the window, or
Browse to it and click Add.  Beam68Prefs refuses unsafe archive paths,
validates the manifest and program, shows name, version, author, licence
and capabilities, asks for confirmation and installs it.  The same ID
with a newer version is an upgrade.

@endnode

@node RULES "Rules"
@{b}Rules for a good plugin@{ub}

  Run on a 68000 with AmigaOS 3.0: no FPU, no MUI, no 020-only code.
  Expect a 4 KB stack: make buffers static.
  Be quick: Beam68 stops waiting after the user's timeout (default 30 s);
    three failures in a row quarantine a plugin.
  One reply line; no console output, no prompts.
  Treat the input as untrusted.
  Close everything you open; leave nothing running.

@endnode
